INT. PLANETARIUM - DOME THEATRE - NIGHT

Rows of reclined seats. The domed ceiling glows with a frozen field of stars. A beam of red light from the projector sweeps slowly across the constellation Orion.

INSPECTOR LINDIWE DUBE (50s, trench coat, notebook) stands at the base of the control console. Beside her, FERDINAND ACHEBE-LARSEN (60s, planetarium director, bow tie) wrings his hands. Over by the exit door, ROLAND PIKE (30s, security guard, flashlight, mint-green uniform) shifts from foot to foot. Slumped across the console under a blanket lies the body of a night technician.

DUBE
Mr. Achebe-Larsen, when did you find her?

FERDINAND
At closing. Around ten.

DUBE
And you touched nothing?

FERDINAND
Nothing. I only draped the blanket.

DUBE
That is touching something.

FERDINAND
Inspector, I could not leave her exposed.

Dube kneels. Lifts the blanket. Studies the hands, the collar, the tiny bruise at the base of the neck. She glances at the console, then up at the false stars.

DUBE
Orion is upside down.

FERDINAND
It is?

DUBE
The projector is calibrated for the southern sky. Someone changed it.

Ferdinand's hands tremble. He steps back, bumping a seat.

ROLAND
Maud never touched the settings. She told me that herself, Mr. Ferdinand.

FERDINAND
Roland, please.

DUBE
Mr. Pike. You were on the door tonight?

ROLAND
Yes, ma'am. I signed everyone out. Only Mr. Ferdinand and Maud were left.

Ferdinand and Roland look at each other. Neither blinks. Dube looks from one to the other and writes one line, underlines it twice.

Her notebook slips. Dube and Roland both stoop to catch it, collide, freeze, both apologize at once.

DUBE
Sorry.

ROLAND
My fault entirely.

She retrieves it herself.

DUBE
Mr. Achebe-Larsen, who has keys to the projector room?

FERDINAND
Only myself and Maud.

DUBE
Then I suggest you tell me where you were at nine fifteen.

The projector hums. A shooting star arcs across the dome, perfectly timed, perfectly empty.

FERDINAND
Inspector. Is that a question?

DUBE
It is the only one I have left.
